docs/plans/2026-06-18-001-feat-twofield-surface-weighted-dissipation-plan.md:55:- **KTD5 — Separate water/splash velocity cap, with FP-safety (supporting).** The surface crown's peak velocity must survive the cap (web scenes currently `max_speed = 12`), but `params.max_speed` is **global** — also used by `drag_fold` (`coupling.wgsl:292`) and `solid_update` (`plasticity.wgsl:420`). Raising it globally would perturb grain/solid dynamics. So introduce a **separate water-splash cap** applied only on the water transfer path (p2g/g2p water clamp sites in `transfers.wgsl`), leaving the global `max_speed` (drag/solid) unchanged. **Re-derive the fixed-point overflow headroom against the water cap** (headroom ∝ `8192/cap`, `common.wgsl:117`) and keep the encode-time magnitude clamp as the backstop; gate with cap-hit-rate + FP-saturation probes (extend `tests/twofield_water.rs`). Not a blanket removal.
docs/plans/2026-06-18-001-feat-twofield-surface-weighted-dissipation-plan.md:56:- **KTD6 — Scope: transfer lever only; stay two-field.** Two-field is kept for its speed/UX (user decision). The incompressibility-pressure lever (over-pack R9, over-buoyant coffee R13, the impact-**absorption** half of "mush") and jet geometry (R2) are a **separate pressure follow-up** — the in-repo `dbg.w` two-sided density-target is the planned path there (already built; compliance diagonal already rejected; a global Poisson risks the checkerboard/operator-inconsistency that killed main's solver against thin perf headroom). Not in this plan.
