docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:13:> **Revised + Codex-APPROVED over 3 /deliberate rounds.** Key changes: drag is landed **before** exclusion is removed; the drag is a **symmetric capped-pair, Jacobi-subiterated** Darcy impulse (aggregate-bounded + resolution-normalized; the implicit/no-overshoot property comes from the bounded blend, **not** a literal `exp()`); retention = **density-target porosity floor + strong drag** (no `∇α_s` body force); calibration uses **multiple banded observables**; buoyancy/drag **double-counting** is avoided; the wake criterion is **drag-only, normalized, decaying**; anti-tunneling gates are sharpened.
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:19:The fix is the textbook **unresolved (semi-resolved) SPH-DEM coupling** (Robinson et al. 2013; Macklin "Unified Particle Physics" 2014): keep PBF for water and rigid XPBD contact for sand-sand, and couple the two phases through (a) the **volumetric solid-fraction field** `α_s` the solver already computes (water density targets `ρ₀·(1−α_s)`, so water packs into pore volume) **backed by an explicit retention floor** so it actually resists free-fall, and (b) a **physically-grounded Darcy drag as a symmetric capped-pair impulse, Jacobi-subiterated** (implicit/non-overshooting via the bounded blend) strong enough to both set drainage *and* mobilize the bed surface. Only **after** that drag is landed and characterized is the old exclusion removed. Water particles are made genuinely finer than grains, with a **wider coupling kernel** so the porosity/drag field stays smooth.
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:27:- **What's wrong:** Water saturates/swells the grounds (the scalar absorption pass works) but the bed shows zero mechanical agitation even at max pour velocity. Diagnosed root causes: the soft water↔grain exclusion (mass-biased, locking-prone) and the ~1000×-too-weak drag (`src/solvers/xpbd/coupling.wgsl`).
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:41:- **R2.** Replace the velocity-blend drag with a **Darcy** relative-velocity drag (viscous only; Re < ~12) as a **symmetric capped-pair, Jacobi-subiterated (implicit), aggregate-normalized** impulse — momentum-conserving and dissipative, resolution-invariant, strong enough that pour-zone impulse **exceeds** surface-grain contact/yield resistance (mobilizes) while bulk impulse stays **below** fluidization.
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:44:- **R5.** Grain **sleep/wake** is reconciled with the strong drag via a **dimensionless/normalized** wake criterion (impulse relative to grain weight, or local flow speed over a minimum duration) with explicit `fluid_impulse` decay: the pour mobilizes surface grains; a dry or saturated-static bed settles without creep.
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:46:- **R7.** Conservation gates hold (momentum drift bounded, drag KE non-increasing, water volume conserved incl. saturated tail). Wetting/extraction/fines scalar layers remain functionally unchanged on top.
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:54:- **KTD-2 — Retention = density-target porosity floor (static) + strong drag (dynamic); no separate body force; remove exclusion only after drag lands.** `α_s` density modulation alone is **not** a hard barrier (a sparse/under-neighbored water particle can advect through, pool below, or ghost in high-α_s zones — Codex r1.1). But a separate `∇α_s` body force is the wrong fix (Codex r2.3/r2.6): it adds another pressure/volume-force channel that fights the buoyancy decoupling and needs its own conservation exception, and a "near-saturation drag limit" can't retain at zero velocity. The reframe: **static retention is moot — at zero relative velocity water is not advecting, so there is nothing to retain.** A resting saturated column is held by PBF **incompressibility** + a **minimum-porosity floor** in the water density target (`target = ρ₀·(1−min(α_s, 1−ε_floor))`, pure PBF, static, momentum-conserving) so water cannot over-pack into grain space; a *moving* wetting front is slowed to the **Darcy velocity by the strong drag** (KTD-3) — percolation, not free-fall. So retention needs **no new force**: density floor + drag. Sequencing: **land the drag first (U3), remove exclusion under it (U2)**, gated by a "no free-fall through a static bed" + bounded-flux test (Codex r1.2/r1.12). If the floor+drag combination empirically still leaks, a *conservative* capillary term is a deferred contingency — not in this plan.
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:55:- **KTD-3 — Darcy drag = symmetric capped-pair blend, Jacobi-subiterated (the existing scaffold, correctly sized).** The round-1 "per-pair `exp()` × aggregate normalization" was self-contradictory (Codex r2.1): a per-pair exponential with a receiver-local norm breaks the mirrored equal-and-opposite property. The solver's **existing** drag structure already resolves symmetry + aggregate-bounding + non-overshoot correctly — keep it, fix only the coefficient. Per pair: `s_ij = min(β_max/max(N_i,N_j), harmonic(β_i,β_j)·V_pair·dt)`, applied with the opposite-effective-mass split `s_ij·(m_j/(m_i+m_j))·(vf_j − vf_i)` on i and its exact negation on j, summed over neighbors, **Jacobi-subiterated** K times (refreshing `vel_frozen`). This is (a) **momentum-conserving** — `s_ij` is symmetric (harmonic combiner) and the opposite-mass split is antisymmetric; (b) **aggregate-normalized** — the `β_max/N` cap bounds `Σ_j s_ij ≤ β_max < 1` per subiter, so adding neighbors (wider `h_c`) never over-damps; (c) **implicit / non-overshooting** — each subiter is a bounded convex blend (`s < 1`) toward the relative velocity, and K subiters approach the exponential/implicit limit `1 − (1−s)^K ≈ 1 − e^{−Ks}` *without a literal `exp()`*. Per-grain Darcy rate `β_i = 150·μ·(1−ε_i)²/(ε_i³·d²)` (Kozeny–Carman, already `porosity_drag_factor ∝ d⁻²`; Ergun inertial dropped — Darcian). **The original bug was simply that β was ~1000× too small, so `s` was rate-limited at ~5e-4 instead of cap-limited at `β_max/N`.** Size β (and `β_max`) so a fine/low-permeability bed is cap-limited → strong aggregate drag. **Resolution-normalize** so per-grain total drag is invariant as water is refined (Codex r1.11) — `β` is per-unit-volume and the cap is per-neighbor, so verify invariance with the two-resolution test (U3) before U5.
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:59:- **KTD-7 — Buoyancy separate AND not double-counted with drag (Codex r1.10/r2.6).** Buoyancy stays `−V·∇P` over the PBF `λ` field (density-aware `grain_buoyancy_factor`); drag forces grains only from **relative velocity**. Because KTD-2 drops the `∇α_s` body force, there is **no third pressure channel** to conflict — the only pressure force on grains is buoyancy, the only velocity force is drag, cleanly separated. Gate: a hydrostatic saturated bed at rest has zero net grain acceleration beyond buoyancy balance; a flowing bed receives drag from relative velocity only.
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:60:- **KTD-8 — `coupling_scale` lane allocation is explicit; 8-buffer budget held (Codex r1.9/r2.2).** Store exactly: `x = β_i` (per-particle Darcy rate from local ε_i), `y = N_i` (opposite-phase neighbor count, for the `β_max/N` cap). The drag gather reads both sides' `(β, N)` and forms `s_ij = min(β_max/max(N_i,N_j), harmonic(β_i,β_j)·V_pair·dt)` — so it needs neither `α_s` nor `ε` nor a separate aggregate at gather time (ε is consumed only when `β_i` is computed in `compute_coupling_scale`). No pass needs `α_s`, `ε`, and `β` simultaneously; add no bindings to the already-8/8 drag passes; never bit-pack two quantities into one lane.
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:61:- **KTD-9 — Normalized, decaying, DRAG-ONLY wake criterion (Codex r1.7/r2.5).** Replace the raw `fluid_impulse > wake_threshold` (scales with dt/subiters/neighbor count) with a **dimensionless** gate (drag impulse relative to grain submerged weight, or local relative-flow speed sustained over a minimum duration) with explicit decay. Crucially, the wake accumulator must be **drag-only** — today `fluid_impulse` is written by *both* `drag_grain` and `buoyancy_grain` (coupling.wgsl:309, 353), so a hydrostatic (zero-flow) saturated bed could wake from buoyancy. Separate the wake signal so buoyancy does **not** contribute to it.
