docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:39:- R3. Water percolates through the bed as relative two-phase flux: Darcy flux scales with K(φ)·head and grind d², infiltration at the free-water/bed interface proceeds at physical rates (no artificial entry barrier), ponding forms only when pour demand exceeds bed capacity and drains on the calibrated timescale after pouring stops.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:40:- R4. Wetting is conserved and mechanical: absorbed water volume transfers 1:1 into grain moisture, grains swell (+15–17% diameter at saturation) with effective mass for momentum, and local porosity/permeability evolve with swelling and compaction.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:44:- R5. Volume (water + absorbed + pore) and momentum are conserved within documented float budgets, including the long-run saturated-tail regime; solute/extraction hooks are out of scope but the conservation pattern must not preclude them.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:64:- KTD-4 — **Infiltration interface physics: mixture incompressibility + wetting-front suction + phase-change absorption.** (a) Continuity is per-phase volume conservation, `∂φ_k/∂t + ∇·(φ_k·v_k) = s_k`, where the s_k carry the absorption/swelling/compaction exchanges; summing gives the projection constraint `∇·(φ_f·v_f + φ_s·v_s) = s_net`, and the projection RHS carries s_net explicitly rather than assuming zero (s_net = 0 only when the exchanges are volume-balanced, which R4 mandates and the conservation gates verify). The momentum side is pinned as equations, not prose: `φ_k·ρ_k·Dv_k/Dt = −φ_k·∇p + ∇·(φ_k·τ_k) + f_drag,k + φ_k·ρ_k·g` (Gao 2018's locally-averaged form), so the intrinsic-velocity pressure correction is `Δv_k = −(Δt/ρ_k)·∇p` — the φ_k cancels; it does NOT scale the velocity update. Pinned invariants: a skeleton at rest under hydrostatic pressure stays at rest (in the rigid-skeleton regime the solid field is not projected into motion — pressure enters the bed as buoyancy and effective stress, i.e., a reaction); submerged grains feel buoyancy through the momentum equation (not just constitutively); a compacting or swelling skeleton displaces pore water instead of silently creating or destroying volume. (b) A capillary suction body force acts on water at the unsaturated wetting front (Green–Ampt; ψ_f ≈ 50–110 mm physical for ~400 µm media, mapped to reduced units), gated by a hydrophobic→wetted transition so the bloom-phase entry delay falls out naturally. (c) Absorption is a moisture phase-change at contact (GIC-style): grid-projected moisture deficit drives transfer from water particles into per-grain moisture, reusing the existing volume-conserving wetting law (`src/models/wetting.rs`) — absorption starts on contact, not after interpenetration.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:65:- KTD-5 — **Bed constitutive model: Klar et al. 2016 Drucker-Prager return map** (3-branch, log-strain, one 3×3 SVD per solid particle per substep) with saturation-dependent cohesion from `src/models/cohesion.rs` (Bishop-style effective stress) and a compaction cap surface for tamping memory (plastic volumetric strain persists and feeds packing fraction → permeability). The tensile-apex branch is what lets crater walls stand and then collapse. **Stress partition is stated once and audited:** total stress = effective stress (DP + cap, with the U5 over-packing solids-pressure guard living INSIDE the contact/effective partition) + pore pressure, and the projection pressure IS the pore pressure — no volumetric mode may be resisted twice by contact pressure, cap pressure, solids-pressure guard, and projection pressure stacking up. The U7 stress-partition audit gates this.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:67:- KTD-7 — **GPU layout discipline:** P2G scatter uses fixed-point `atomic<i32>` accumulation (KEEP.md §3 pattern, FP_SCALE re-validated against the velocity cap with documented overflow headroom); bind groups are split by pass family (transfers / pressure / plasticity / coupling) per `docs/issues/003-split-mpm-bind-groups.md`; every entry point fits the device's storage-buffer request. **Discrepancy to resolve in U1:** `src/utils/gpu.rs` currently requests exactly 9 storage buffers (`NEEDED_STORAGE_BUFFERS`) while AGENTS.md sanctions 16 — the scaffold unit raises the request only if the two-field grid genuinely needs it and verifies browser grants; grid fields are packed into vec4 lanes first.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:69:- KTD-9 — **The validation ladder is the project spine and the execution posture.** Each rung's gate tests are written before that rung's physics (test-first at the gate level, mirroring the analytic-test doctrine: discrete-recurrence references, CPU twins of GPU kernels, set-invariant assertions, headless GPU gating, no `#[ignore]`). A failed gate halts the ladder for redesign — by explicit decision there is no pre-committed fallback architecture. To make the halt implementable rather than a tuning purgatory, each rung declares a knob grid when its gate tests are written, and the grid must enumerate EVERY tunable its gates depend on — for U3: coarse ratio {1/4³, 1/8³} × fine-sweep range × Tait predictor on/off × pressure tolerance; later rungs add timestep/substep cap, suction parameters, drag blend width, solids-pressure stiffness, plastic damping, and boundary/filter coefficients as applicable; exhausting the grid without a pass IS the halt condition, and reaching for any knob outside the grid is a redesign decision, not a retry.
